      <div class="blog-hero-grid">
        <div class="blog-hero-txt">
          
          <h1><span id="hs_cos_wrapper_name" class="hs_cos_wrapper hs_cos_wrapper_meta_field hs_cos_wrapper_type_text" style="" data-hs-cos-general-type="meta_field" data-hs-cos-type="text">REST and REST APIs: A Complete Guide for Developers</span></h1>
          <p class="blog-hero-meta">
            Learn what REST and REST APIs are, the six REST principles, HTTP methods, security patterns, and how to manage REST APIs at scale across gateways.
          </p>

          <div class="blog_details">
            
            <p><span class="a_title">Published:</span> <span class="a_value">June 9, 2026</span></p>
            
          </div>
          <div class="blog_details">
            
            <p><span class="a_title">Author:</span> <a class="a_value" href="https://www.gravitee.io/blog/author/jorge-ruiz" rel="author">Jorge Ruiz</a></p>
            
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-hero-img">
          
          <img src="https://www.gravitee.io/hubfs/Copy%20of%20Gravitee%20Blog-1.png" loading="lazy" alt="Hero Feature Image">
          
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    

    <article class="blog-post-wrapper">
      <div class="blog-post-container">
        <div class="blog-table-of-contents">
          <div class="blog-table-container">

          </div> 
        </div> 
        <div class="blog-post-body">
          <div id="hs_cos_wrapper_module_17544958664612" class="hs_cos_wrapper hs_cos_wrapper_widget hs_cos_wrapper_type_module" style="" data-hs-cos-general-type="widget" data-hs-cos-type="module">
<section class="fun-facts-section">
  <h3 class="fun-facts-header">Fast Facts</h3>

  <div class="facts-container">
    <ul>
<li>REST <span>represents an architectural style for distributed systems, established by Roy Fielding in 2000. It is not a protocol or standard.</span></li>
<li><span>A REST API is any interface complying with REST's six constraints: uniform interface, statelessness, client-server separation, cacheability, layered system, and code on demand (optional).</span></li>
<li><span>REST APIs utilize HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) to manipulate resources identified by URIs, typically swapping JSON.</span></li>
<li><span>"REST API" and "RESTful API" are used interchangeably. "RESTful" suggests stricter compliance with all six constraints.</span></li>
<li><span>In production, REST APIs require more than authentication libraries. They demand gateway-enforced security, federated governance, observability, and lifecycle management.</span><br><br></li>
</ul>
  </div>

  <div class="social-buttons">
    <button class="social-btn copy-btn" onclick="copyCurrentURL()">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-link"></i> Copy Link
    </button>

    <a href="#" class="social-btn twitter-btn" id="twitterShare" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <i class="fa-brands fa-square-x-twitter"></i>
    </a>

    <a href="#" class="social-btn facebook-btn" id="facebookShare" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      <i class="fa-brands fa-square-facebook"></i>
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="copy-success" id="copySuccess">
    Link copied
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Ensure functions are available globally and DOM is ready
  (function() {
    // Copy URL function - made global
    window.copyCurrentURL = async function() {
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(window.location.href);
        showCopySuccess();
      } catch (err) {
        // Fallback for older browsers
        const textArea = document.createElement('textarea');
        textArea.value = window.location.href;
        textArea.style.position = 'fixed';
        textArea.style.left = '-9999px';
        document.body.appendChild(textArea);
        textArea.focus();
        textArea.select();

        try {
          document.execCommand('copy');
          showCopySuccess();
        } catch (fallbackErr) {
          console.error('Could not copy text: ', fallbackErr);
          alert('Unable to copy link. Please copy manually: ' + window.location.href);
        }

        document.body.removeChild(textArea);
      }
    };

    function showCopySuccess() {
      const successMsg = document.getElementById('copySuccess');
      if (successMsg) {
        successMsg.classList.add('show');
        setTimeout(() => {
          successMsg.classList.remove('show');
        }, 3000);
      }
    }

    // Initialize everything when DOM is ready
    function initializeFunFacts() {
      // Get current URL and set up social sharing links
      const currentURL = encodeURIComponent(window.location.href);
      const pageTitle = encodeURIComponent(document.title || 'API Gateway Fun Facts');

      // Set up social sharing URLs
      const twitterShare = document.getElementById('twitterShare');
      const facebookShare = document.getElementById('facebookShare');

      if (twitterShare) {
        twitterShare.href = `https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=${currentURL}&text=${pageTitle}`;
      }

      if (facebookShare) {
        facebookShare.href = `https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=${currentURL}`;
      }

      // Add interactive behavior to fact items
      const factItems = document.querySelectorAll('.fact-item');
      factItems.forEach((item, index) => {
        item.addEventListener('mouseenter', function() {
          this.style.transform = 'translateY(-5px) scale(1.02)';
        });

        item.addEventListener('mouseleave', function() {
          this.style.transform = 'translateY(0) scale(1)';
        });
      });
    }

    // Run when DOM is ready
    if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
      document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', initializeFunFacts);
    } else {
      initializeFunFacts();
    }
  })();
</script>

</div>
          <span id="hs_cos_wrapper_post_body" class="hs_cos_wrapper hs_cos_wrapper_meta_field hs_cos_wrapper_type_rich_text" style="" data-hs-cos-general-type="meta_field" data-hs-cos-type="rich_text"><p>Many developers can define REST in a single line. Fewer can articulate why their REST API halts under pressure, fails a compliance check, or malfunctions when integrating with a Kafka stream or an AI agent. The distance between knowing REST and operating REST APIs in production exceeds what most content suggests.</p>
<!--more-->
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>This manual addresses both areas. You will receive the basics of REST and REST APIs, the six constraints defining the style, daily HTTP methods, and REST comparisons against options like SOAP, GraphQL, and event-driven models. You will also encounter production realities: securing REST APIs at scale, governing them across various gateways, and linking them to the agent-driven systems your company is beginning to construct.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>What is REST?</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST signifies Representational State Transfer. It is an architectural style for distributed hypermedia systems, presented by Roy Fielding in his 2000 doctoral thesis at UC Irvine. Fielding was a lead author of the HTTP specification, and REST evolved directly from that effort.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A few things REST is not, since confusion remains constant:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span>REST is not a protocol. HTTP is a protocol. REST is a collection of constraints applicable when building systems atop protocols like HTTP.</span></li>
<li><span>REST is not a standard. No governing body certifies an API as RESTful. Compliance exists on a spectrum.</span></li>
<li><span>REST is not identical to HTTP. While most REST APIs operate over HTTP, REST could theoretically apply to other protocols.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST defines a set of architectural constraints that, when adhered to, yield systems with specific traits: scalability, evolvability, visibility, portability, and reliability. Fielding argued the web succeeded because it followed these constraints. Applying the same constraints to APIs yields similar advantages.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>The practical outcome is that REST provides a shared vocabulary and design conventions. When two developers claim they are building a REST API, they usually mean the same thing, despite no committee enforcing it.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>What is a REST API?</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A REST API is any interface conforming to REST's architectural constraints. Practically, this means an API exposing resources at URIs, using HTTP methods to operate on them, and exchanging representations (usually JSON, sometimes XML) over HTTP.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A standard REST API call appears as follows: a client sends GET https://api.example.com/orders/12345 and the server returns a JSON document detailing order 12345. To create a new order, the client sends a POST containing order data in the request body. To update it, use PUT or PATCH. To remove it, DELETE. This pattern remains consistent across every REST API you have used.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST APIs dominate the pattern for web and mobile backends, microservice communication, and SaaS integrations. If you called a public API in the last decade, it was likely REST. Stripe, GitHub, Twilio, Salesforce, and most cloud providers expose REST APIs as their main interface.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>You will see "REST API" and "RESTful API" used interchangeably. A technical distinction exists (covered in a dedicated section below), but in daily conversation they mean the same thing.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>The Six Principles of REST</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Fielding defined six constraints an API must follow to qualify as RESTful. Five are mandatory. The sixth is optional. Together they generate the properties REST is known for.</span></p>
<h3 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Uniform interface</span></strong></h3>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>The uniform interface is the core constraint distinguishing REST from other architectural styles. It contains four sub-constraints:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span>Resources are identified by URIs.</span></strong><span> Every significant entity (an order, a user, a product) possesses a stable URI.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Resources are manipulated through representations.</span></strong><span> The client interacts with a representation of the resource (JSON, XML, HTML), not the resource directly.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Messages are self-descriptive.</span></strong><span> Each request and response carries sufficient metadata (HTTP method, status code, headers, content type) to be understood without external context.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>HATEOAS (Hypermedia as the Engine of Application State).</span></strong><span> Responses include links indicating what actions are available next to the client. Most APIs labeled REST do not implement this fully.</span></li>
</ul>
<h3 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Client-server separation</span></strong></h3>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>The client and server hold distinct responsibilities. The client owns the user interface and user state. The server owns data storage and business logic. This separation allows them to evolve independently. You can rewrite the mobile app without touching the backend, or migrate the database without altering the API.</span></p>
<h3 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Statelessness</span></strong></h3>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Every request from a client must contain all information the server needs to process it. The server does not store session state linked to a specific client between requests. Authentication, for instance, gets sent with every request (typically as a bearer token in the Authorization header) rather than being held in a server-side session.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Statelessness enables REST APIs to be horizontally scalable. Any server can handle any request, allowing load balancers to route traffic anywhere without sticky sessions.</span></p>
<h3 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Cacheability</span></strong></h3>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Responses must declare whether they can be cached and for how long. Cache directives travel within HTTP headers (Cache-Control, ETag, Expires). Executed well, caching drastically reduces server load and client latency. Executed poorly, it serves stale data and creates debugging nightmares.</span></p>
<h3 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Layered system</span></strong></h3>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A client cannot tell if it is communicating directly with the origin server or an intermediary. Load balancers, CDNs, reverse proxies, and API gateways sit between client and server without changing the contract. This is exactly the constraint enabling API gateways, which we will revisit.</span></p>
<h3 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Code on demand (optional)</span></strong></h3>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Servers can optionally ship executable code to clients (JavaScript, applets) extending client functionality. This is the single constraint REST treats as optional, and in practice, almost no one uses it for API design.</span></p>
<div id="hs_cos_wrapper_widget_6fd94f2a-0232-4850-b849-03b44b00d4d8" class="hs_cos_wrapper hs_cos_wrapper_widget hs_cos_wrapper_type_module" style="" data-hs-cos-general-type="widget" data-hs-cos-type="module"><section class="api-platform-section">
