</section></div>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>How REST APIs Work</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A REST API call follows a predictable lifecycle. The client constructs an HTTP request targeting a specific URI, the server processes it, and a response returns with a status code and (usually) a body.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A request comprises four parts:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span>Method</span></strong><span>: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, and a few others.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>URI</span></strong><span>: identifies the resource, e.g., /api/v1/orders/12345.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Headers</span></strong><span>: metadata such as Authorization, Content-Type, Accept, Cache-Control.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Body</span></strong><span>: optional payload for methods like POST, PUT, and PATCH. Typically JSON.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A response has three components:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span>Status line</span></strong><span>: HTTP version plus a status code (200 OK, 201 Created, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error).</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Headers</span></strong><span>: metadata regarding the response (content type, length, cache directives).</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Body</span></strong><span>: the resource representation, typically JSON.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>The server authenticates the request, validates input, applies business logic, fetches or modifies data, and returns the result. Between client and server you often find intermediaries: a load balancer, a CDN, an API gateway enforcing rate limits and authentication, and observability tools capturing metrics. The layered system constraint enables that stack.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>JSON has become the de facto representation format. It is lighter than XML, native to JavaScript, and well-supported in every language. XML still appears in regulated industries and older systems.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>HTTP Methods in REST APIs</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST maps HTTP methods to CRUD operations. The mapping is conventional, not enforced, but you should follow it.</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span>GET</span></strong><span>: Read a resource. Safe (does not modify state) and idempotent (calling it many times has the same effect as calling it once). Responses are cacheable.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>POST</span></strong><span>: Create a resource, or trigger an action that doesn't fit the other methods. Not idempotent. Calling POST twice typically creates two resources.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>PUT</span></strong><span>: Replace a resource entirely. Idempotent. PUT-ing the same payload ten times leaves the resource in the same state as PUT-ing it once.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>PATCH</span></strong><span>: Partially update a resource. Often idempotent in practice, though the spec doesn't require it.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>DELETE</span></strong><span>: Remove a resource. Idempotent. Deleting an already-deleted resource should return the same result.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Idempotency matters because networks are unreliable. A client receiving no response cannot tell if the request succeeded or failed. If the method is idempotent, the client can safely retry. If not (POST), the client risks creating duplicate resources. That is why payment APIs often require an Idempotency-Key header on POST requests.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A few less common methods round out the set: HEAD (like GET but returns headers only), OPTIONS (discover what methods a resource supports, used heavily in CORS preflight), and TRACE (rarely used, often disabled for security reasons).</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>REST API vs RESTful API: Is There a Difference?</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>In day-to-day conversation, no. The terms get used interchangeably.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>The technical distinction lies in strictness. A RESTful API adheres strictly to all of REST's required constraints, including HATEOAS. A REST API is the looser, everyday usage covering anything exposing resources over HTTP with JSON and using HTTP methods conventionally.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Leonard Richardson proposed a four-level maturity model capturing the spectrum:</span></p>
<div align="left">
<div data-hs-responsive-table="true" style="overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 98%; margin: 4px 0px 0px;">
<table style="border-collapse: collapse; border: solid; width: 100%; height: 324px;"><colgroup><col style="width: 33.393%;" width="208"><col style="width: 33.393%;" width="208"><col style="width: 33.214%;" width="208"></colgroup>
<thead>
<tr style="height: 33.1875px;">
<th style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; height: 33.1875px; text-align: center;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Level</span></p>
</th>
<th style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; height: 33.1875px; text-align: center;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Description</span></p>
</th>
<th style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; height: 33.1875px; text-align: center;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Example</span></p>
</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr style="height: 71.3906px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>0</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Single URI, single method (usually POST). Effectively RPC over HTTP.</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Many older SOAP-style APIs</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 71.3906px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>1</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Multiple resources at distinct URIs, but still one method.</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Some legacy APIs</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 71.3906px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>2</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Resources plus correct use of HTTP methods and status codes.</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 71.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Most APIs called REST</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 72.3906px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 72.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>3</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 72.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Level 2 plus HATEOAS (hypermedia controls in responses).</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; padding: 16px; height: 72.3906px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>GitHub API (partially); most APIs don't reach this level</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Most APIs in production sit at Level 2. They use HTTP methods correctly, return appropriate status codes, and structure URIs around resources, but they don't embed hypermedia links. Strictly speaking, those APIs aren't fully RESTful, but the industry calls them REST anyway and the term has stuck.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>For practical purposes, treat the terms as synonyms. If someone insists on the distinction, they are usually arguing about HATEOAS.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Benefits of REST APIs</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST's benefits flow directly from its constraints:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span>Scalability</span></strong><span>: Statelessness means any server can handle any request. Horizontal scaling becomes trivial. Caching reduces backend load further.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Flexibility</span></strong><span>: Client-server separation lets the front end and back end evolve independently. You can rewrite a mobile app without changing the API, or refactor the backend without breaking clients.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Platform independence</span></strong><span>: REST runs on HTTP and uses JSON, which every modern language, runtime, and platform supports. A Python backend can serve a JavaScript front end and a Swift mobile app from the same API.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Simplicity</span></strong><span>: Standard HTTP verbs and readable URIs make REST APIs easy to learn, document, and debug. You can explore most REST APIs with curl or a browser.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Tooling</span></strong><span>: The ecosystem is massive. OpenAPI for specifications, Postman for testing, every API gateway on the market for management. You won't find tooling gaps.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>These benefits explain why REST became the default. They also explain why REST has held its position against successive challengers like SOAP, GraphQL, and gRPC.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>REST API Authentication and Security</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A REST API in production needs authentication, authorization, transport security, and protection against abuse. The mechanisms have matured into a small set of well-known patterns:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span>API keys</span></strong><span>: A static secret sent in a header (Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt; or X-API-Key: &lt;key&gt;). Simple to implement, easy to leak. Best for server-to-server traffic with low sensitivity, or as a first step for public APIs.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>Basic Authentication</span></strong><span>: Username and password base64-encoded in the Authorization header. Effectively obsolete for new APIs. Still appears in legacy systems.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>OAuth 2.0</span></strong><span>: Token-based authorization framework. The client obtains a short-lived access token from an authorization server and presents it on each request. Standard for delegated access, third-party integrations, and most modern public APIs. OAuth 2.1 consolidates best practices and removes deprecated flows.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>JWT (JSON Web Tokens)</span></strong><span>: A token format, often used as the access token in OAuth flows. Self-contained and signed, so the API can verify them without a database lookup. Watch the expiration and revocation story carefully.</span></li>
<li><strong><span>mTLS (mutual TLS)</span></strong><span>: Both client and server present X.509 certificates during the TLS handshake. Strong machine-to-machine authentication. Common in financial services, healthcare, and any system that needs FAPI-grade security.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>That covers authentication. You also need authorization (what is this caller allowed to do?), rate limiting (how do you stop one consumer from overwhelming the API?), input validation (how do you reject malformed or hostile requests?), and observability (how do you know what's happening in production?).</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Here is the part most tutorials skip: implementing all of this in application code, per service, doesn't scale. If you have ten REST APIs, you have ten places where authentication logic lives, ten places where rate limiting gets configured, and ten places where the inevitable security update has to ship. Inconsistency is how shadow APIs and audit failures happen. This is where API gateways earn their place, enforcing authentication, OAuth flows, mTLS, rate limiting, and threat protection centrally so application teams stop writing the same security logic over and over.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>REST vs SOAP, GraphQL, and Event-Driven APIs</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST isn't the only option. The alternatives each solve a specific problem REST handles poorly.</span></p>
<div align="left">
<div data-hs-responsive-table="true" style="overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 97%;">
<table style="border-collapse: collapse; border: 1px solid; width: 100%; height: 549px;"><colgroup><col style="width: 25.072%;" width="156"><col style="width: 25.072%;" width="156"><col style="width: 25.072%;" width="156"><col style="width: 25.072%;" width="156"></colgroup>
<thead>
<tr style="height: 33.1953px;">
<th style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; height: 33.1953px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Style</span></p>
</th>
<th style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; height: 33.1953px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Format</span></p>
</th>
<th style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; height: 33.1953px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Best for</span></p>
</th>
<th style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; height: 33.1953px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Tradeoffs</span></p>
</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr style="height: 107.781px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 107.781px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><strong><span>REST</span></strong></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 107.781px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>JSON over HTTP</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 107.781px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Resource-oriented APIs, broad client diversity</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 107.781px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Over-fetching, under-fetching, request-response only</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 126.977px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 126.977px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><strong><span>SOAP</span></strong></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 126.977px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>XML over HTTP (or other transports)</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 126.977px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Regulated industries, contract-heavy integrations, WS-* security</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 126.977px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Verbose, heavier tooling, slower to evolve</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 146.172px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 146.172px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><strong><span>GraphQL</span></strong></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 146.172px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Single endpoint, query language</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 146.172px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Client-shaped queries, complex object graphs, mobile apps minimizing payload</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 146.172px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>More complex caching, query cost analysis required, learning curve</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
<tr style="height: 127px;">
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 127px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><strong><span>Event-driven (Kafka, MQTT, WebSocket, SSE)</span></strong></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 127px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Binary or text streams, depends on broker</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 127px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Real-time data, pub/sub, high-frequency events, async workflows</span></p>
</td>
<td style="vertical-align: top; border: 1px solid #000000; width: 25%; padding: 15px; height: 127px;">
<p style="line-height: 1.2; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;"><span>Different mental model, separate infrastructure, harder to govern</span></p>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>SOAP remains common in banking, insurance, and government systems where contracts and WS-Security matter. GraphQL has found a strong niche in front-end-heavy applications where the cost of multiple REST round trips becomes painful. Event-driven patterns dominate when you need real-time data, push semantics, or high-throughput streams.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>Real architectures combine these styles. A typical enterprise stack today runs REST APIs for&nbsp;</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>synchronous interactions, Kafka or MQTT for event streams, and occasionally GraphQL at the edge for mobile clients. Governing all of them through separate tools is how policy drift starts. Unifying them under one control plane is what platform teams increasingly look for.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>When to Use REST APIs</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST fits well when:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span>Your data model is resource-oriented (users, orders, products, documents).</span></li>
<li><span>Request-response is acceptable. Most CRUD-style workloads qualify.</span></li>
<li><span>You need broad client compatibility across web, mobile, and server-to-server.</span></li>
<li><span>Caching at the HTTP layer would benefit you (read-heavy workloads).</span></li>
<li><span>You want maximum tooling and ecosystem support.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST is a poor fit when:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span>You need real-time push from server to client. Use WebSocket or Server-Sent Events.</span></li>
<li><span>You're streaming high-frequency events to many consumers. Use Kafka, MQTT, or similar.</span></li>
<li><span>Clients need fine-grained control over response shape to avoid over-fetching. Consider GraphQL.</span></li>
<li><span>You need continuous bi-directional communication, such as chat or collaborative editing. Use WebSocket.</span></li>
<li><span>Interactions span long-running workflows with multiple participants. Consider event-driven or workflow-based patterns.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>A pragmatic rule: start with REST. Add other styles when you hit a problem REST genuinely can't solve, not because the alternative is fashionable.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Managing REST APIs at Scale</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>One REST API is easy. Fifty REST APIs across four teams, three clouds, and two acquired companies is where things break. The challenges shift from how do I build a REST API to how do I keep all of them secure, discoverable, observable, and consistent.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>API gateways</span></strong><span> solve the first layer of the problem. A gateway sits between clients and your REST APIs and enforces cross-cutting concerns centrally: authentication (OAuth 2.0, JWT validation, mTLS, API keys), rate limiting and quotas, request and response transformation, threat protection, caching, and observability. Instead of every service implementing OAuth flows in code, the gateway handles it once and applies the policy uniformly.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Federated governance</span></strong><span> is where most North American enterprises actually live. You are probably not running a single gateway. You have AWS API Gateway in front of some Lambda functions, Apigee from an earlier modernization effort, Azure API Management for the analytics platform, and maybe IBM API Connect or Kong somewhere in the back. Ripping all of that out is rarely realistic. What you need is a control plane that can discover, catalog, and govern APIs across all of those gateways without forcing a migration. This is what federated API management delivers: one set of policies, one developer portal, one audit trail, applied consistently over the multi-gateway environment you already have. Gravitee's Federated API Management approach works this way, federating governance across AWS API Gateway, Apigee, Azure API Management, IBM API Connect, Confluent, and Solace alongside Gravitee's own gateway.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Developer portals</span></strong><span> address the discovery problem. A REST API nobody can find is a shadow API waiting to happen. A portal gives internal developers, partners, and external consumers a self-service catalog with documentation, interactive testing, subscription plans, and usage analytics. When portals span every gateway in your environment, you stop the duplicate-build pattern where a team writes a new service because they didn't know one already existed.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Lifecycle management</span></strong><span> ties it all together. APIs need to be designed, deployed, versioned, observed, and eventually retired. Doing that through Terraform or a Kubernetes operator (rather than clicking through UIs) is what lets platform engineering teams keep up as the API footprint grows.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>REST APIs in Modern Architecture</span></strong></h2>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST APIs used to have two consumers: browsers and mobile apps. That is no longer true. Today, REST APIs are consumed by microservices calling each other, by event-driven workflows that fan out to dozens of downstream services, and increasingly by AI agents.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>The AI consumer is the part most articles miss. When an agent built on a framework like LangChain or invoked through Anthropic's Claude or OpenAI's tool-use APIs needs to take an action in the real world, it does so by calling an API. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) has emerged as a standard way for agents to discover and invoke tools, and most of those tools are wrappers around REST APIs.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>This changes the governance picture. A human consumer typing into a UI moves slowly and gets rate-limited by usage patterns. An agent can fire hundreds of requests per second, chain calls across systems, and operate with broader permissions than any individual user. The questions that used to be theoretical (which agent called this API? was it authorized? did it exceed its budget?) are now operational.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>The interesting question for 2026 isn't what is REST. It's how do you govern REST APIs when an autonomous agent is the consumer. That involves giving agents distinct identities (not shared service accounts), enforcing tool-level scopes (not blanket access), and observing agent behavior with the same rigor you apply to human users. Gravitee's Agent Tool Server exposes existing REST APIs as MCP-compliant tools without backend changes, so the REST APIs you already have can become governed agent tools through configuration rather than rewriting.</span></p>
<p style="line-height: 1.2;"><span>REST isn't going anywhere. The protocol is too entrenched, the tooling too mature, and the developer mental model too well-established to be displaced. What's changing is the surrounding architecture: more consumers, more protocols alongside it, and stricter governance demands.</span></p>
<h2 style="line-height: 1.2;"><strong><span>Frequently Asked Questions</span></strong></h2>
