<div id="accordionGroup" class="accordion">
  
  <h3>
<button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-trigger" aria-controls="sect1" id="accordion1id">

      <span class="accordion-title">
        Is a REST API the same as a RESTful API?
        <span class="accordion-icon"></span>
      </span>
    </button>
  </h3>
  <div id="sect1" role="region" aria-labelledby="accordion1id" class="accordion-panel">
    <p>In everyday usage, yes. Technically, "RESTful" implies strict adherence to all six REST constraints, including HATEOAS. Most APIs called REST sit at Level 2 of the Richardson Maturity Model, meaning they use resources and HTTP methods correctly but skip hypermedia. The industry treats the terms as synonyms.</p>
  </div>
  
  <h3>
<button type="button" aria-expanded="false" class="accordion-trigger" aria-controls="sect2" id="accordion2id">

      <span class="accordion-title">
        What is a simple example of a REST API?
        <span class="accordion-icon"></span>
      </span>
    </button>
  </h3>
  <div id="sect2" role="region" aria-labelledby="accordion2id" class="accordion-panel" hidden>
    <p>Public examples include the GitHub API (GET https://api.github.com/users/octocat), the Stripe API (POST https://api.stripe.com/v1/charges), and most public weather services. Each exposes resources at URIs, uses HTTP methods conventionally, and returns JSON.</p>
  </div>
  
  <h3>
<button type="button" aria-expanded="false" class="accordion-trigger" aria-controls="sect3" id="accordion3id">

      <span class="accordion-title">
        Can REST APIs handle real-time data?
        <span class="accordion-icon"></span>
      </span>
    </button>
  </h3>
  <div id="sect3" role="region" aria-labelledby="accordion3id" class="accordion-panel" hidden>
    <p>REST is request-response by design, so it isn't the right tool for true real-time push. For real-time scenarios, use WebSocket for bi-directional channels, Server-Sent Events for server-to-client push, or an event broker like Kafka or MQTT for high-throughput streams. A modern API platform should govern REST alongside these protocols rather than treating them as separate worlds.</p>
  </div>
  
  <h3>
<button type="button" aria-expanded="false" class="accordion-trigger" aria-controls="sect4" id="accordion4id">

      <span class="accordion-title">
        How do you secure a REST API in production?
        <span class="accordion-icon"></span>
      </span>
    </button>
  </h3>
  <div id="sect4" role="region" aria-labelledby="accordion4id" class="accordion-panel" hidden>
    <p>OAuth 2.0 (or 2.1) for delegated access, mTLS for high-assurance machine-to-machine traffic, and JWT for self-contained tokens. Add rate limiting, input validation, and gateway-level enforcement so the same policies apply to every API. Implementing authentication libraries per service doesn't scale and creates inconsistency that audits catch.</p>
  </div>
  
  <h3>
<button type="button" aria-expanded="false" class="accordion-trigger" aria-controls="sect5" id="accordion5id">

      <span class="accordion-title">
        What is the difference between REST and GraphQL?
        <span class="accordion-icon"></span>
      </span>
    </button>
  </h3>
  <div id="sect5" role="region" aria-labelledby="accordion5id" class="accordion-panel" hidden>
    <p>REST exposes multiple resources at distinct URIs and uses HTTP methods to operate on them. GraphQL exposes a single endpoint and lets clients send queries that specify exactly which fields they want. REST is simpler and benefits from HTTP caching. GraphQL reduces over-fetching for clients with complex needs. Most architectures use both: REST for general-purpose APIs and GraphQL where client query flexibility matters.</p>
  </div>
  
  <h3>
<button type="button" aria-expanded="false" class="accordion-trigger" aria-controls="sect6" id="accordion6id">

      <span class="accordion-title">
        Do REST APIs require an API gateway?
        <span class="accordion-icon"></span>
      </span>
    </button>
  </h3>
  <div id="sect6" role="region" aria-labelledby="accordion6id" class="accordion-panel" hidden>
    <p>Not strictly. A handful of internal services can run without one. Beyond that, a gateway becomes essential for centralizing authentication, rate limiting, observability, and policy enforcement. Once you're running APIs across multiple teams, gateways, or clouds, federated API management becomes the practical way to keep governance consistent without ripping out your existing infrastructure.</p>
  </div>
  
</div>





